      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.048,
            0.419,
            0.065
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.088,
            0.845,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": "？ Using this observation, mentally calculate how much 15% of the values in the table would be."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.141,
            0.801,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": "Suppose you have to mentally calculate the following percentages of some value: 75%, 90%, 70%, 55%. How would you do it? Discuss."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.818,
            0.134,
            0.904,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.202,
            0.793,
            0.224
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "The FDP Trio — Fractions, Decimals, and Percentages"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.238,
            0.845,
            0.301
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Example 2: We can find 50% of a value by multiplying $\\frac { 1 } { 2 }$ with the value. Will multiplying the value by 0.5 also give the answer for 50% of the value?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.308,
            0.321,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": "Yes, since $\\textstyle { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } = 0 . 5 .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.711,
            0.308,
            0.843,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": "50% of 24 = 12 0.5 × 24 = 12"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.381,
            0.336,
            0.616,
            0.365
          ],
          "content": "50\\% = \\frac{50}{100} = \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{0.5}{1} = 0.5.",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.369,
            0.845,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": "？ Similarly, to find 10% of a quantity, what decimal value should be multiplied?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.424,
            0.441,
            0.448
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Complete the following table:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.461,
            0.839,
            0.585
          ],
          "content": "| Per cent | 50% | 100% | 25% | 75% | 10% | 1% | 5% | 43% |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Fraction | 50100 |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| Decimal | 0.5 |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_table_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.627,
            0.522,
            0.647
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity: How Close Can You Get?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.651,
            0.824,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": "Make a pair. Each of you choose a number. Suppose, the numbers chosen are a and b. Share your numbers with each other. Both of you should estimate the percentage equivalent to the fraction $\\frac { a } { b }$ (where a < b) and announce your answers by a fixed time, say, 5 seconds. The one whose estimate is the closest wins this round. Play this for 10 rounds."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.81,
            0.845,
            0.867
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Example 3: The maximum marks in a test are 75. If students score 80% or above in the test, they get an A grade. How much should Zubin score at least to get an A grade?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.873,
            0.847,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "We can find 80% of 75 in different ways, using our understanding of fraction and decimal multiplication, as well as of proportionality."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.094,
            0.914,
            0.114,
            0.935
          ],
          "content": "8"
        }
      ]
    }
